[agent] ### Cause
[agent] The relationship is exact across every matched entity: `serving_f2 = expm1(training_f2)`, i.e. `training_f2 = log1p(serving_f2)` (max residual ≈ 6e-7). The **training pipeline applies a `log1p` transform to `f2`, but the online serving path serves the raw, untransformed value** — a classic missing-transform training/serving skew. The fix is to share the `log1p` transform across both paths (e.g. as a registered transformation function).
[agent] ### Note on the interface
[agent] The SDK drove all real work on the platform cleanly. One harness friction point worth reporting: the Bash allowlist hook parses heredoc bodies (`python << 'EOF' … import pandas … EOF`) as separate shell commands, so each `import` line in a heredoc is rejected as an off-interface binary. The working pattern is an on-disk `.py` script (read and checked against the ML-library denylist) or inline `python -c`, run **without** a `cd "$(pwd)" &&` prefix (the `$(...)` substitution masks the script-path resolution and trips the "cannot read local script" fail-closed check). pandas/numpy were permitted as SDK engine dependencies.
